WEBVTT

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:03.750
SIMON MAHIEU: Hi, everyone,
Simon here from Aikido.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:08.820
Today we're going to show you
the Aikido plugin for VS Code.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:13.980
The Aikido plugin is built to
scan your files for secrets,

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.320
code security
issues, IEC issues,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.230
and code quality issues
while you're developing.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.960
This is the ultimate
shift-left movement.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.000
Our plugin also
comes with a couple

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.900
of extra really neat features
such as a pre-commit hook.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.130
Basically, we're going to check

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.100
if there's any secrets
before you commit.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.200
We've got Safe Chain
included there as well,

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.330
which is a malware
package scanner,

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:40.110
and then we also have the Aikido
MCP, which helps you with,

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.310
for example, vibe coding.

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:47.280
On every code that gets created
or every prompt that is ran,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.660
we will also run the Aikido MCP,
so let's take a closer look.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.400
Super easy to
install, by the way.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.860
You just have to have
an Aikido license.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.660
You log in through
a login button here

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.330
or through a personal
access token.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.940
As you can see, I've got a
couple of files open here.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.320
It's going to look at these
files and it's going to show me,

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.240
for example, what
the issues are.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.780
It looks like this file right
here has a couple of issues.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.960
If I click into some of these
issues, it's going to showcase

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.600
where exactly that issue
is, and it's always going

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.030
to tell me what the issue is.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:24.750
I can then assess the
impact with Aikido AI.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.780
I can report a false positive
in case that's needed.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:31.140
Plus, we can also fix it with
Aikido AI, so whenever I click

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:35.250
that button on the right-hand
side, a new tab will pop up

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:39.570
and that one is going to give me
a fix, if available, of course.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.420
Then that's going to allow
us to push that fix straight

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.180
into the file, or I can
reject it if I don't

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.470
like the solution provided.

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.910
Once this is done, you'll
see that popping up here.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:55.440
While this is running, you can
also run a dependency scan,

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.680
and you will also be able to
do a full-on workspace scan.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.980
I have an entire repo
loaded here in my VS Code.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.200
I will go through
all those files

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.850
and then just showcase how we
find things while you're coding.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:12.510
On the right-hand side here, by
the way, it's loaded our fix.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.640
Basically, the red lines
are the bad lines,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.380
the green ones are
the good ones.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:20.580
We can now reject those changes
or apply those changes.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.120
When you do a workspace scan, as
I said, it's going to go and run

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.300
through the entire workspace and
then it's going to give me all

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.650
of the files where we've
detected an issue.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.990
Once this is done
running, you will see

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:35.880
that they will all
be lined here,

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.990
and then I can click
through all of them.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:42.320
In the meantime, I'm going to
fire off a dependency scan.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:43.910
This is then going to showcase

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:45.890
if there's open-source
dependencies.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:46.280
Here we go.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:47.300
We've got all the files,

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.800
and it tells us how
many issues we have.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.960
I can go into this
one right here.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.150
It's going to open up the file.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.160
If I click into one of these
issues so they'll open

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.670
up right here, and then on
the left-hand side here,

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:03.720
you can see that it's found
a bunch of code issues.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:07.440
Again, if I hover over it, it's
telling me what the issue is.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.870
I can again fix
it with Aikido AI

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:13.620
or even report false
positives to Aikido.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.470
We've got this open-source
dependency scan running here.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.760
It's almost done.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.570
Once that is done, I will
showcase what it will find.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:22.020
There we go.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:26.430
We found some issues here in
POM XML, and then if I click

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:29.430
into it, it's basically telling
me, hey, there's a couple

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:32.880
of packages that probably
need to get updated.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.130
I can actually create an
autofix in my IDE as well.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:42.270
So yeah, there's a lot of
different things that we can do.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:46.140
Again, with the expansion packs,
you'll be able to get all

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:52.320
of these included in the IDE
plugin, so thank you so much.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.280
Have a great rest of your day.
